# many_initialized_globals.tcyr — v6.3.41 regression for the initialized-globals
# per-compilation-unit cap (the `gvar_toks` deferred-init table).
#
# Filed 2026-07-03 (thoth): a large first-party app that vendors several dist
# bundles into ONE compilation unit crossed the old 1024-slot ceiling —
# `error:<file>:<line>: too many initialized globals (max 1024)` — forcing the
# ecosystem to carve artificially-lean distlib profiles purely to fit a
# consumer's global budget. v6.3.41 raises the cap 1024 -> 4096 by relocating
# gvar_toks off 0x198000[8192] to the reclaimed 0x729000[32768] band (the .27
# output_buf gap, below TS@0x800000). The count gvar_cnt stays at 0x19A000.
#
# COUNTING RULE this exercises: only a NON-literal top-level initializer
# (`var g = <call/ident/expr>;`) consumes a gvar_toks slot. A bare integer
# literal (`var g = 5;`) takes the static-init fast path (_vgsi_base) and does
# NOT count; enum members do not count either. This fixture uses call-init
# globals (`var gN = seed();`) — exactly the "mutable lazy-init pointer/scratch"
# shape (crc/huffman/deflate tables) that tipped sankoch's bundle over 1024.
#
# 1100 call-init globals > the old 1024 cap (would error at global 1026 on a
# pre-.41 compiler) and < the new 4096 cap (compiles clean on .41+).

include "lib/syscalls.cyr"
include "lib/assert.cyr"

fn seed(): i64 { return 3; }

var g0 = seed();
var g1 = seed();
var g2 = seed();
var g3 = seed();
